  To find the highest common ancestor (HCA) of two nodes in a binary tree, you can use a recursive approach. The basic idea is to traverse both trees from the root node, and whenever you reach a node that is common to both trees, mark it as the current HCA and continue traversing the trees until you reach the end.
Here is an example of how you could implement this function in JavaScript:
```
function findCommonAncestor(root1, root2) {
  // Create a stack to keep track of the nodes to traverse
  const stack = [root1, root2];

  // Recursively traverse the trees and find the HCA
  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const current = stack.pop();
    if (current === null) {
      // If we reach the end of one of the trees, return the current HCA
      return current;
    }
    // If the current node is common to both trees, mark it as the HCA and continue traversing
    if (current.left === null && current.right === null) {
      // If the current node has no children, it must be the HCA
      return current;
    }
    // Add the children of the current node to the stack
    stack.push(current.left);
    stack.push(current.right);
  }

  // If we reach the end of both trees without finding an HCA, return null
  return null;
}
```
This function takes two parameters: `root1` and `root2`, which are the roots of the two binary trees to find the HCA of. It returns the highest common ancestor of the two trees, or `null` if no HCA is found.
